# Intent: Fable review risk 1 (2026-11-16). Patch 160 let the boot loader judge the first boot of a wirelessly installed image, so any reset before the image was kept (the vendor camera panic on about half of the cold USB boots) rolled a good image back, and a unit on a trip with no host rolled back at 60 s. Now the app marks the image good at its first boot (the boot loader's blind rule cannot fire) and keeps its own trial record in flash (namespace fwtrial): a crash reset in a boot where the camera had not yet settled (2 s after board init returns) is a free pass, at most three; a crash after that is a strike and the second strike rolls back; a power loss or software restart is no fault; the trial ends good at 20 s with the host link up or, as the second path, at 60 s with no crash and no host needed; a trial still open at 120 s (a stuck main loop, a camera init that never returns) rolls back. The rule is a pure header tested with scripted boots. Events: firmware_update_validated now carries path host or quiet; firmware_update_rolled_back is new.
# Placement: unit (the robot decides about its own boot slot)
# Upstream: main/hal/{hal.cpp,maipai_update.cc,maipai_update.h} (ours, patches 159 and 160); new file hal/maipai_update_core.h
# Check after a flash: host/tests/firmware/update_core_test.c passes on the PC. On the unit after a wireless update: the log says "on trial", then "reached the host; keeping it" at about 25 s; with the host off it says "ran 60 seconds with no crash; keeping it"; a power cycle inside the first minute keeps the new image.

